+/*
+ * RedwoodGPU on negroni: the Adreno 730 for programs to draw with.
+ *
+ * bsd/dev/negroni_adreno.c wakes the GPU and, with boot-args negroniadreno=22
+ * negroniadreno_keep=1, keeps it on and publishes the resource "NegroniGPUKept"; the IOResources
+ * personality RedwoodGPU (iokit/KernelConfigTables.cpp) then starts this service. The user
+ * client speaks two ABIs on the same connection (docs/gpu-abi.md in the project repo; user side
+ * userland/negronigl/drm/ngl_abi.h):
+ *
+ *   scalar   selectors 10-16, the ones of ios-sailfish's SweetGPU (its tools/sweetgl/sweet_drm.c
+ *            and our NGL_ABI_SCALAR): bo new/free/import, submit, wait, info, cpu prep.
+ *   msm      selector = DRM_COMMAND_BASE + DRM_MSM_* (0x40-0x4c) or 0x09 (GEM_CLOSE), the Linux
+ *            msm structure in and out (slop 0118, HackiOS redwood-drm, NGL_ABI_MSM_STRUCT), plus
+ *            0x7e: an IOSurface as a buffer (struct redwood_drm_surface), and 0x49: the caller's own
+ *            pages as a buffer (struct redwood_drm_userptr, VK_EXT_external_memory_host; msm's unused
+ *            GEM_SVM_NEW number). What Linux does with mmap() is IOConnectMapMemory64 with the memory
+ *            type = the buffer's handle.
+ *
+ * Memory. A buffer of our own is wired kernel memory (IOBufferMemoryDescriptor), mapped CACHED
+ * in the kernel and in the program: a write-combined mapping of memory XNU manages is a second,
+ * mismatched view of pages its physical aperture maps cacheable, and changing the pages' cache
+ * mode rewrites that aperture (ios-sailfish: froze boot 0150a; ios-flame: data lost). The kernel
+ * keeps the GPU's view right instead: at each submission it cleans the pages the CPU wrote since
+ * the last one (the pmap's modified bit) out to memory, and CPU_PREP drops the CPU's lines over
+ * what the GPU wrote. With boot-arg negronigpu_pool=MiB (default 0: off) msm GEM_NEW buffers that
+ * ask for MSM_BO_WC come from RAM XNU does not manage (negronigpu_poolbase, default 0x980000000:
+ * above Mu's "Kernel RAM" and XNU's 3 GiB, System RAM to Linux), mapped write-combined everywhere:
+ * coherent without cache maintenance, which Vulkan's HOST_COHERENT memory (Turnip) needs.
+ * IOSurface imports are the surface's own pages (NegroniSurface.cpp), cleaned at import. Host
+ * imports (0x49) are the caller's pages, wired while the handle lives, cleaned at import; the CPU
+ * keeps its own cached mapping (rules as for imports: docs/gpu-abi.md section 6).
+ *
+ * GPU addresses: one SMMU context (the 3D core's), 39-bit, 4K pages. Default: one set of tables
+ * for every client (as HackiOS and sailfish); each client may reserve a window of its own
+ * (MSM_PARAM_VA_START/VA_SIZE) and place buffers in it (MSM_INFO_SET_IOVA, Turnip's userspace
+ * iova); otherwise the kernel picks. Boot-arg negronigpu_pt=1 (patch 0233): every client has its
+ * own tables (negroni_gpu_vm_*, bsd/dev/negroni_adreno.c), so its commands reach only its own
+ * buffers and the kernel's part (0..4 GiB); the window is then the same 16 GiB in every client.
+ *
+ * Fences: one ring, one sequence for every client and queue. A submission's fence is done when
+ * the 3D core has written it (negroni_gpu_done). A wait that sees the completed fence not move
+ * for 2 s while work is outstanding declares the GPU stopped: from then on submissions fail
+ * (kIOReturnNotReady), waits fail, buffers that may still be in use stay wired (never freed
+ * under a GPU that might wake up), MSM_PARAM_FAULTS answers 1 (Vulkan: device lost).
+ * Patch 0287: first the GPU is reset and started again (negroni_gpu_recover, at most
+ * negronigpu_recover_max times in 60 s): the fences it cut off read done but failed; a client wait
+ * on such a fence answers kIOReturnError, MSM_PARAM_FAULTS counts the recoveries (Turnip: device
+ * lost), and the submit queue's fault count counts those that cut off the client's own work.
+ * Only when the recovery is off, fails or comes too often, the GPU stops as before.
+ *
+ * Lifetime (slop 0121-0124): the service exists from the "NegroniGPUKept" resource on; it holds
+ * nothing (no memory, no thread, no mapping) until a program opens it, so its existence alone
+ * touches no memory and no register. Per client, everything lives in the user client and is
+ * given back in clientClose (also called when the program dies) and, as a second line, free().
+ */
